["complications","A jaundiced patient with biliary obstruction develops fever, rigors, and right-upper-quadrant pain. Which complication requires urgent evaluation?","Fever and biliary pain accompany obstructive jaundice. What complication is concerning?","Acute cholangitis","Obstructed bile ducts can become infected.","Uncomplicated lactose intolerance","This does not explain the biliary infectious pattern.","An isolated ligament injury","This is unrelated to obstructed bile.","A stable asymptomatic hiatal hernia","This does not account for fever with biliary obstruction."],
["tests","A patient has jaundice. Why are bilirubin fractions and liver-related enzymes assessed?","What do fractionated bilirubin and related enzymes help distinguish?","Different patterns of bilirubin production, hepatic injury, and obstruction","Laboratory patterns help direct evaluation of jaundice's cause.","The exact cause of every jaundice case without any history or imaging","Labs alone do not establish every cause.","Whether the patient has an esophageal motility disorder only","These studies evaluate a different process.","Whether all intestinal bacteria are absent","That is not their diagnostic purpose."],
["management","A patient with jaundice from a confirmed obstructing bile duct stone asks whether treating the skin color alone is sufficient. Which response is best?","What is the central treatment principle for obstructive jaundice?","Address the underlying obstruction and monitor complications","Jaundice is a sign requiring cause-directed treatment.","Treat jaundice as an independent disease with no cause","The underlying mechanism matters.","Ignore fever because yellow skin explains it completely","Fever may signal infection.","Assume every jaundiced patient needs the same antiviral","Not all jaundice is viral."]
