["complications","An untreated cholecystitis patient develops worsening pain, peritoneal signs, and imaging concern for wall necrosis. Which complication is suspected?","Gallbladder inflammation progresses to wall necrosis and peritoneal irritation. What complication is concerning?","Gangrene or perforation of the gallbladder","Severe inflammation can compromise and disrupt the wall.","An uncomplicated resolved gallstone episode","The deterioration argues against resolution.","Primary esophageal achalasia","This does not explain gallbladder necrosis.","Stable uncomplicated lactose intolerance","This does not cause these peritoneal findings."],
["tests","A patient has suspected acute cholecystitis. Which imaging findings on ultrasound support inflammation?","Which ultrasound pattern supports acute cholecystitis?","Gallstones with wall thickening and pericholecystic fluid","These findings support an inflamed gallbladder.","An isolated simple renal cyst","This does not establish gallbladder inflammation.","Normal bowel gas without gallbladder assessment","This does not characterize the gallbladder.","Only a narrowed esophageal lumen","This assesses another region."],
["management","A patient with acute cholecystitis is admitted with vomiting and fever. Which plan is appropriate during surgical evaluation?","What care supports an acutely inflamed gallbladder while definitive treatment is planned?","Prescribed fluids, analgesia, and infection-directed treatment when indicated","Supportive treatment and timely source management address the acute illness.","A large fatty meal to force gallbladder contraction","This can worsen symptoms.","Delay all evaluation until fever disappears spontaneously","Complicated disease requires timely care.","Treat only chronic constipation and ignore biliary findings","This fails to address the acute problem."]
